{"id":"int-8cff4b6c316e6ae520c4168ebd4ee359","kind":"field_change","created_at":"2026-09-17T15:26:04.960320361Z","actor":"Will Winder","issue_id":"muscle-mass-chart-88s","extra":{"field":"status","new_value":"closed","old_value":"open","reason":"Unified setPhaseEndAge in scenario.js to handle the last-phase case by auto-splitting (shortens the phase, appends a copy of it as a new trailing phase to AGE_MAX) instead of a separate splitPhase() midpoint-split function. Removed the Split button and the always-static 'through 90' label in TimelineEditor.svelte; the last phase's end age is now a normal editable input. Verified via a scratch script that repeated edits correctly chain auto-splits and that non-last phases still just move the shared boundary."}}
{"id":"int-a329bf9bc7d49fadfdbcf5ad816ae73d","kind":"field_change","created_at":"2026-09-17T15:29:58.03985764Z","actor":"Will Winder","issue_id":"muscle-mass-chart-4kg","extra":{"field":"status","new_value":"closed","old_value":"open","reason":"Split the per-year update into a natural (age-driven) component and a training component. The natural component still uses full growth / retention-scaled decline depending on the baseline's own direction. The training component (that year's increase in the bonus) is now added unconditionally, at any age, so switching to higher-intensity training later in life produces real regrowth rather than merely slowed loss. Verified numerically: sedentary preset still tracks the baseline exactly (0 diff), lifelong trainer is essentially unchanged (~2pt difference from residual bonus convergence), and a sedentary-until-50-then-high-strength-training scenario now rises from 80.8 at 50 to a local peak of 85.1 at 60 before gently declining -- a genuine reversal, not just reduced loss."}}
